# Trust-region Newton

Trust-region Newton minimizes smooth scalar objectives using a gradient and
second-order information. It limits each quadratic-model step to a ball and
adjusts that ball according to how well the model predicts objective reduction.
You can supply a full Hessian or use Hessian-vector products without forming a
matrix.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/trust_region/struct.TrustRegion.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                                    |
  | ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
  | Objective   | A smooth scalar function                                       |
  | Evaluations | `CostFunction`, `Gradient`, and `Hessian` or `HessianProduct`  |
  | Search      | Local, from one starting point                                 |
  | Constraints | Unconstrained; the trust-region ball is an internal step limit |
  | Parameters  | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64`                   |

## When to use it

Consider this solver when you can supply useful second-order information. The
matrix-free mode is useful when forming and storing a Hessian is too expensive.
If you only have a gradient, compare [L-BFGS](../../quasi-newton/l-bfgs/). If
the objective is a residual sum of squares, compare the [nonlinear least-squares
solvers](../../#nonlinear-least-squares).

[Nocedal and Wright (2006)](#ref-nocedal-wright-2006), Chapter 4 and Algorithm
4.1, describe the trust-region framework. Its convergence results require
assumptions on the objective and model quality. A finite run does not certify a
global minimum, and a small gradient alone need not exclude a saddle.

## How it works

Each iteration approximately minimizes `gᵀp + 0.5 pᵀHp` subject to `‖p‖ ≤ Δ`.
The ratio of actual to predicted reduction determines whether to accept the
trial and shrink or grow `Δ`. Rejected trials solve the model again at a smaller
radius.

The default Steihaug strategy uses truncated conjugate gradient. It stops at the
boundary or follows a detected negative-curvature direction to it. Other
strategies trade subproblem cost against model accuracy:

  | Strategy       | Model operations                | Behavior                                                                   |
  | -------------- | ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
  | `Steihaug`     | Hessian-vector products         | Truncated CG; supports matrix-free mode                                    |
  | `CauchyPoint`  | Hessian-vector products         | Minimizes along the negative gradient; supports matrix-free mode           |
  | `Dogleg`       | Matrix products and Cholesky    | Combines Cauchy and Newton steps; falls back to Cauchy for a non-SPD model |
  | `MoreSorensen` | Eigendecomposition and Cholesky | Near-exact secular solve with explicit hard-case handling                  |

[Moré and Sorensen (1983)](#ref-more-sorensen-1983) describe the near-exact
subproblem method. Even a globally solved quadratic subproblem does not make the
outer nonlinear search global.

## Example

This quadratic supplies Hessian-vector products directly. The example uses
matrix-free Steihaug with a gradient tolerance and an iteration budget.

<!-- compile: solver-trust-region-newton -->
```rust
use basin::{CostFunction, Executor, Gradient, HessianProduct, TrustRegion};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Quadratic;

impl CostFunction for Quadratic {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok(0.5 * (x[0].powi(2) + 100.0 * x[1].powi(2)))
    }
}

impl Gradient for Quadratic {
    type Gradient = Vec<f64>;

    fn gradient(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<Self::Gradient, Self::Error> {
        Ok(vec![x[0], 100.0 * x[1]])
    }
}

impl HessianProduct for Quadratic {
    fn hessian_product(
        &self,
        _: &Self::Param,
        v: &Self::Param,
    ) -> Result<Self::Param, Self::Error> {
        Ok(vec![v[0], 100.0 * v[1]])
    }
}

fn main() {
    let solver =
        TrustRegion::matrix_free().with_absolute_gradient_tolerance(1e-10);
    let result = Executor::from_start(Quadratic, solver, vec![5.0, 1.0])
        .max_iter(100)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(result.cost() < 1e-16);
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Match the strategy to the derivative interface

`TrustRegion::new()` uses a full `Hessian` and Steihaug.
`TrustRegion::with_subproblem(Dogleg)` selects another matrix strategy.
`TrustRegion::matrix_free()` requires `HessianProduct` instead;
`matrix_free_with(CauchyPoint)` is also available. Dogleg and Moré–Sorensen
require a matrix and cannot run in matrix-free mode.

`FiniteDiff` can synthesize Hessian-vector products on every backend. With
ndarray, exact-Hessian mode needs a supplied `Array2` Hessian because the
adapter cannot construct one. Numerical derivatives add evaluations and
approximation error; set budgets and tolerances accordingly.

### Configure resolution and stopping separately

`with_radius` and `with_max_radius` control the model's step scale; `with_eta`
controls acceptance. Scale parameters so one Euclidean radius is meaningful.
These settings are algorithm controls, not accuracy tolerances.

Gradient and observed progress checks are disabled by default. Enable
`with_absolute_gradient_tolerance` or `with_relative_gradient_tolerance` for an
optimality check. A finite non-positive model prediction reports convergence
only for an exactly zero computed gradient; otherwise it produces
`NumericalNoProgress`. A collapsed radius alone does not establish stationarity.
Keep iteration and evaluation budgets on the executor.

### Budget Hessian-vector products

Exact mode reuses the Hessian across radius retries at one point. Matrix-free
mode must repeat CG products when it retries with a smaller radius. Count and
limit `EvaluationKind::HessianProduct` separately when those calls dominate the
cost.

## Implementation

The solver publishes matching parameters, cost, and gradient through
`FirstOrderState`. It owns the trust radius and rejection history. Fresh
initialization restores the configured radius and reevaluates the start; exact
solver-and-state checkpoints retain the current history. Use
`Executor::run_with_solver` and `radius()` to inspect the retained radius.

Supported parameter types are `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. All four
dense backends provide the operations for every shipped matrix strategy.
Matrix-free mode binds no matrix type. Pure-Rust configurations work on WASM
without a BLAS/LAPACK provider; optional LAPACK acceleration has separate
platform requirements.

